  "doctrine": [
    {
      "topic": "ascent-and-exaltation",
      "statement": "Psalm 68:19 addresses the one who ascended on high and took captives. Ephesians 4:8 takes up that ascent-language and applies it to the Messiah who descended and ascended, giving gifts (4:9–11). The passage develops the meaning of that ascent through his action, rather than making every occurrence of עָלָה a reference to him.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "ref": "Psa.68.19"
        },
        {
          "ref": "Eph.4.8"
        },
        {
          "ref": "Eph.4.9"
        },
        {
          "ref": "Eph.4.10"
        },
        {
          "ref": "Eph.4.11"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "note": "Duplicate spelling inventories p.cs.ae/af (עֹלָה), p.cs.ai/aj (עֶלְיוֹן), p.cs.an/ao (מַעֲלָה), and p.cs.ap/aq (תְּעָלָה) share occurrences: one key per combined word is used. The key totals can therefore include a sibling meaning or a verbal participle and are not sense totals. The rare hithpael at Jer 51:3 and ascent/burnt-offering at 1Ki 10:5 need review; the noun’s ascent sense is instead anchored at Ezk 40:26. Proper names and redirect-only rows are omitted. עַלְוָה “injustice” at Hos 10:9 is assigned elsewhere under עלוה/עול, not taken from the name Alvah. Aramaic/Syriac enter-verbs contracted to על belong with עלל, not with “ascend”; they are not included on spelling alone. Syriac עלוא “waking state” has no Peshitta token and is omitted. Leaf, pestle, water-channel and healing are retained without speculative semantic bridges or unrelated cognates. Left out for עלל: Biblical Aramaic עִלָּה and the Peshitta's ܥܠܬܐ “cause, ground of accusation” (Dan 6:5–6; Mat 19:3), which answer to Arabic عِلَّة ʿilla “cause, pretext”, of the root علل, not علو; they go with the עלל entry, as the enter-verbs do."
